In fpush(), a char was converted to double by Fpushc or Fpushuc according
to the UNSIGNED bit of its type. A plain char has no such bit but is
unsigned in bcc, and a signed char gets the same "c", so the helper could
not tell them apart. Chars and shorts are now widened to int first, with
extend(), which knows the difference (it is what assignment to an int uses),
and pushed with Fpushi or Fpushui.

--- a/bcc/floatop.c
+++ b/bcc/floatop.c
@@ -167,6 +167,15 @@
 	    return;
 	pointat(source);
     }
+    else if (scalar & (CHAR | SHORT))
+    {
+	/* Widen to int as extend() does elsewhere: the type character
+	 * alone cannot tell a plain char (unsigned in bcc) from a signed
+	 * one, so Fpushc could only get one of them right. */
+	extend(source);
+	load(source, DREG);
+	scalar = source->type->scalar;
+    }
     else if (scalar & DLONG)
 	load(source, OPREG);
     else
